{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>You shipped a security header and the page went quiet</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team adds <code>Content-Security-Policy: script-src 'self'</code> to their static site's response headers, a well-known recommendation from every security checklist. They deploy it, reload the homepage, and the mobile nav menu no longer opens. The analytics snippet stopped firing. A little <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block that used to set up a dropdown is just gone.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nothing crashed loudly. The browser console shows a line reading something like <code>Refused to execute inline script because it violates the following Content Security Policy directive: "script-src 'self'"</code>, and every inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block and <code>onclick="..."</code> attribute on the page simply never ran.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      <code>script-src 'self'</code> means exactly what it says: only scripts loaded from the page's own origin, as external files, are allowed to execute. It says nothing about where the script physically sits in the markup, it's about how the browser knows to trust it. Inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> content and inline event handler attributes are both, by CSP's default rules, untrusted by definition, because an attacker who can inject markup into the page (the exact threat CSP exists to contain) can just as easily inject an inline script tag as an external one.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is CSP doing its job correctly, not a bug to route around. The entire value of a script-src policy against XSS comes from the assumption that an attacker's injected payload is unauthenticated markup, if inline script executed by default, an attacker's injected <code>&lt;script&gt;alert(document.cookie)&lt;/script&gt;</code> would run exactly as freely as your own. The fix isn't loosening the policy back to <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>, that reopens the exact hole CSP was added to close, it's giving your own legitimate inline scripts a way to prove they're yours.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Nonces and hashes: proving an inline script is yours, not an attacker's</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      There are two real fixes, and "add <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> to make the errors stop" is not one of them, it disables the exact protection you were adding. The first fix is to stop using inline script at all: move the code into an external <code>.js</code> file and load it with <code>&lt;script src="/app.js"&gt;&lt;/script&gt;</code>, which <code>script-src 'self'</code> already allows. The second fix, for cases where inline script genuinely can't move (a small per-page config value, a CMS that injects a snippet), is to explicitly allowlist that one inline block with a nonce or a hash.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A nonce-based policy looks like <code>script-src 'nonce-r4nd0mVaLu3'</code>, and every inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> tag you actually authored gets a matching <code>nonce="r4nd0mVaLu3"</code> attribute, generated fresh on the server for every single response. Since an attacker's injected payload was never given that response's nonce, their inline script gets blocked while yours executes. A hash-based policy (<code>script-src 'sha256-abc123...'</code>) works without a server generating anything per-request: it allowlists a specific inline script by the exact hash of its contents, so it only works for inline script whose text never changes between deploys.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Neither mechanism extends to inline event handler attributes like <code>onclick="doThing()"</code>, only <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> elements and (with <code>'unsafe-hashes'</code> explicitly added) certain inline handlers get nonce/hash treatment, and browser support for hashing handler attributes is inconsistent. In practice, migrating away from inline <code>on*</code> attributes to <code>addEventListener</code> in an external script is the more portable fix, and it also removes a whole separate legacy code smell independent of CSP.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The CMS snippet that broke the checkout page</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A marketing team rolled out a CSP header site-wide after a security audit flagged its absence. Most pages were fine, the JS was already bundled into external files. But the checkout page had a small inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block, injected by the CMS template, that read a discount code from a URL parameter and displayed it. With CSP live, that script silently stopped running, and the discount banner never appeared, quietly costing conversions for two days before anyone noticed a pattern.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody had touched the checkout page's own code, the header change alone was enough to break it, because the CMS's template inserted the value as literal inline script rather than through the site's external bundle.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The team's first instinct was to add <code>'unsafe-inline'</code> back to unblock the banner immediately, which worked, and also silently disabled CSP's script protection sitewide, not just on that one snippet. A follow-up review caught it before the next deploy: they instead generated a per-request nonce server-side, injected it into that one CMS-templated script tag, and added <code>'nonce-&lt;value&gt;'</code> to the policy, leaving every other injected script still blocked.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The retrospective's real finding was that "add the CSP header" had been treated as a single deploy rather than an audit: nobody had inventoried which pages actually depended on inline script or handler attributes before turning the policy on globally. They added a pre-deploy step scanning built HTML for <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> blocks with no <code>src</code> and <code>on*=</code> attributes, specifically so the next policy tightening wouldn't ship blind again.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

