{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Report-only mode, and the parts a nonce doesn't cover</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Before enforcing a new policy, ship it as <code>Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only</code> first. It logs violations to the console (and to a reporting endpoint, if you configure one) without actually blocking anything, so you can find every inline script and handler the real policy would break before it breaks them in production.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A nonce must be freshly random per response, reusing the same nonce across requests (or worse, hardcoding one in a static HTML file) defeats the mechanism entirely: an attacker who can read your page's source can read a static nonce and put it on their own injected script too. This is why nonce-based CSP genuinely requires a server generating each response, a fully static site without per-request generation has to lean on hash-based CSP instead, or move everything to external files.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Hash-based CSP has its own footgun: the hash is computed over the exact bytes of the script's contents, including whitespace, so a minifier or template engine that reformats inline script differently between builds silently breaks the allowlist on every deploy unless the hash is regenerated as part of that same build. Also watch <code>style-src</code> the same way, inline <code>style="..."</code> attributes and <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> blocks are gated by CSP too, and teams that only audit <code>script-src</code> before enabling a policy often get a second, quieter breakage on inline styles they didn't think to check.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

